{"causes": ["The institutionalization and expansion of the medieval Inquisition provided the framework for Puigcercós's authority and role.", "The Dominican Order's foundational mission to combat heresy positioned its friars, including Puigcercós, at the forefront of inquisitorial activities.", "The persistent presence of diverse religious and intellectual currents within the Crown of Aragon necessitated a powerful figure to enforce Catholic orthodoxy."], "effects": ["Bernat de Puigcercós's actions contributed to the consolidation of inquisitorial power and procedure within the Crown of Aragon.", "His prosecutions led to the suppression of various perceived heresies and the enforcement of religious conformity across his broad jurisdiction.", "The precedents set during his tenure influenced the operational methods and institutional strength of the Inquisition in subsequent centuries."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "dominican-order", "sourceName": "Dominican Order", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "bernat-de-puigcercos", "targetName": "Bernat de Puigcercós", "context": "The Dominican Order's mission to combat heresy profoundly shaped Bernat de Puigcercós's career and role as an inquisitor."}, {"sourceSlug": "bernat-de-puigcercos", "sourceName": "Bernat de Puigcercós", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "inquisition-in-the-crown-of-aragon", "targetName": "Inquisition in the Crown of Aragon", "context": "Bernat de Puigcercós served as the General Inquisitor, actively participating in and leading the Inquisition's efforts within the Crown of Aragon until 1342."}, {"sourceSlug": "crown-of-aragon", "sourceName": "Crown of Aragon", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "barcelona", "targetName": "Barcelona", "context": "Barcelona, a significant urban center, was a key city within the Crown of Aragon where Bernat de Puigcercós likely conducted much of his inquisitorial work."}, {"sourceSlug": "bernat-de-puigcercos", "sourceName": "Bernat de Puigcercós", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "suppression-of-heterodoxy-in-aragon", "targetName": "Suppression of Heterodoxy in Aragon", "context": "Bernat de Puigcercós's rigorous inquisitorial campaigns directly caused the increased suppression of heterodox beliefs and practices throughout the Crown of Aragon."}, {"sourceSlug": "king-peter-iv-of-aragon", "sourceName": "King Peter IV of Aragon", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "bernat-de-puigcercos", "targetName": "Bernat de Puigcercós", "context": "King Peter IV of Aragon, despite occasional tensions, generally collaborated with Bernat de Puigcercós to maintain religious and social order within his realms."}], "places": [{"name": "Crown of Aragon", "role": "Jurisdiction"}, {"name": "Barcelona, Catalonia", "role": "Inquisitorial Base"}, {"name": "Valencia, Crown of Aragon", "role": "Area of Activity"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Bernat de Puigcercós", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "bernat-de-puigcercós"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q50600947", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bernat_de_Puigcerc%C3%B3s"], "tags": ["Religious Figures", "comparative-religion", "doctrine-development", "cultural-diffusion"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Bernat de Puigcercós played a critical role in strengthening the institutional power and reach of the Inquisition across the Crown of Aragon, setting precedents for religious conformity that influenced later centuries. His actions directly contributed to the suppression of diverse religious expressions and solidified the Dominican Order's authority in medieval Iberian society.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-11T12:10:54Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29150933435", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Bernat de Puigcercós (O.P.) was a Catalan Dominican friar that lived during the 14th century. He was general Inquisitor of the Crown of Aragon.", "newValue": "Bernat de Puigcercós (O.P.), a formidable Catalan Dominican friar, emerged as a pivotal figure in the ecclesiastical landscape of the 14th century, serving as the General Inquisitor for the Crown of Aragon. His tenure, culminating in his death in 1342, coincided with a period of intense religious scrutiny and the institutional consolidation of the Inquisition across Iberia. Puigcercós was not merely an administrator but a driving force in defining and enforcing orthodox doctrine, directly influencing the spiritual and intellectual climate of territories spanning Catalonia, Aragon, Valencia, and the Balearic Islands.

During his active years, Puigcercós presided over numerous inquisitorial processes, particularly targeting suspected heresy, Jewish converts (Conversos), and even members of other religious orders deemed heterodox. He played a crucial role in the prosecution of spiritual movements and individuals whose interpretations challenged established Church authority, thereby contributing to the suppression of intellectual dissent. His extensive jurisdiction meant that his decisions had far-reaching implications for individuals and communities, often resulting in severe penalties, including public penance, confiscation of property, and sometimes execution.

